        4
      </entry>
            <entry>
        5
      </entry>
            <entry>
        6
      </entry>
            <entry>
        7
      </entry>
            <entry>
        8
      </entry>
            <entry>
        9
      </entry>
            <entry>
        10
      </entry>
            <entry>
        11
      </entry>
            <entry>
        12
      </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <emphasis>
        \(T(h)\)
      </emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry>
        18
      </entry>
            <entry>
        19
      </entry>
            <entry>
        20
      </entry>
            <entry>
        20
      </entry>
            <entry>
        25
      </entry>
            <entry>
        23
      </entry>
            <entry>
        17
      </entry>
            <entry>
        15
      </entry>
            <entry>
        11
      </entry>
            <entry>
        11
      </entry>
            <entry>
        8
      </entry>
            <entry>
        6
      </entry>
            <entry>
        7
      </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <para id="para-00003">In questions 1 – 3, find the average rate of change for the following intervals. Be prepared to show your reasoning.
</para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">between noon and 1 p.m.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00005">Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para id="para-00006">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00007">1°F per hour. The temperature rose by 1°F in 1 hour.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00008">between noon and 4 p.m. </para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00009">Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para id="para-00010">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00011">1.75°F per hour. The temperature increased by 7°F in 4 hours. The average increase is \(\frac74\) or 1.75. </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00012">between noon and midnight</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00013">Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para id="para-00014">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00015">–0.92°F an hour. I found the slope of the line connecting the two points, and it is \(-\frac{11}{12}\). </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <para id="para-00016">In questions 4 – 6, use the slope formula to find the slope of the line that connects the following points.
</para>
      <!--Q4-->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00017">\((0, 18)\) and \((1, 19)\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00018">Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para id="para-00019">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00020">\(\frac{19-18}{1-0} = 1\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q5-->
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00021">\((0, 18)\) and \((4, 25)\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00022">Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para id="para-00023">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00024">\(\frac{25-18}{4-0} = \frac74\) </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q6-->
      <exercise id="exer-00006">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00025">\((0, 18)\) and \((12, 7)\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00026">Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para id="para-00027">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00028">\(\frac{7-18}{12-0} = \frac{-11}{12}\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q7-->
      <exercise id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00029">Compare your answers to questions 1 – 3 with your answers to questions 4 – 6. Explain what this tells you about the average rate of change and the slope of a line. </para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00030">Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para id="para-00031">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00032">The average rate of change between two points is the same as the slope of the line connecting the 2 points. </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <para id="para-00033">For questions 8 and 9, think back to Mai and Tyler’s disagreement about temperature to solve these problems.
</para>
      <!--Q8-->
      <exercise id="exer-00008">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00034">Use the <term data-store="storename" id="term-00001">average rate of change</term> to show which time period—4 p.m. to 6 p.m. or 6 p.m. to 10 p.m.—experienced a faster temperature drop. </para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00035">Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para id="para-00036">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00037">The temperature dropped faster between 4 p.m. and 6 p.m. For example: In the first interval, the temperature fell 8°F in 2 hours, which is an average drop of 4°F an hour. In the second, it fell 9°F in 4 hours, which is an average drop of 2.25°F per hour.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q9-->
      <exercise id="exer-00009">
        <problem id="prob-00009">
          <para id="para-00038">Use the slope formula to show which line has the greater slope—the line connecting the points that represent the temperature at 4 p.m. and 6 p.m. or the line connecting the points that represent the temperature at 6 p.m. and 10 p.m. </para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00009">
          <para id="para-00039">Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para id="para-00040">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00041">The slope of the line representing the temperature between 4 p.m. and 6 p.m. has a greater slope. The slope of the line connecting the points representing the temperature between 4 p.m. and 6 p.m. is
      \(\frac{17-25}{6-4} = -4\). The slope of the line connecting the points representing the temperature between 6 p.m. and 10 p.m. is \(\frac{8-17}{10-6} = \frac{9}{4}=-2.25\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q10-->
      <exercise id="exer-00010">
        <problem id="prob-00010">
          <para id="para-00042">Compare your answers to question 8 and question 9.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00010">
          <para id="para-00043">Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para id="para-00044">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00045">Finding the average rate of change and using the slope formula give the same results. The average rate of change and the slope of the line between hours 4 and 6 is greater than the average rate of change and the slope of the line between hours 6 and 10. </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
